[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Build Process, Bundling, and Webpack in NativeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding the build process and bundling mechanism in NativeScript is essential for optimizing app performance, reducing startup time, and shrinking binary sizes. NativeScript relies heavily on Webpack to bundle JavaScript, TypeScript, CSS, and asset files into optimized packages for iOS and Android."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The NativeScript Build Pipeline"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you run commands like 'ns run android' or 'ns build ios', the NativeScript CLI executes a series of compilation and bundling steps behind the scenes."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Transpilation: TypeScript and modern JavaScript are transpiled down to target versions supported by the native platform engines (V8, JavaScriptCore)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Resource Processing: XML layouts, CSS stylesheets, and images are processed and prepared for native consumption."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Webpack Bundling: All application modules are resolved, tree-shaken, and bundled into optimized distribution files."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Native Compilation: The native projects (Gradle for Android, Xcode for iOS) build the final binary using the bundled assets."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Role of Webpack in NativeScript"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"NativeScript uses Webpack as its primary module bundler. The default configuration is exposed in the 'webpack.config.js' file at the root of your project. Webpack performs tree-shaking to eliminate dead code, minifies scripts, and generates source maps for debugging."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Customizing the Webpack Configuration"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you need to add custom plugins, loaders, or resolve paths, you can modify 'webpack.config.js'. NativeScript exports a pre-configured function that you can extend."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const webpack = require("@nativescript/webpack");

module.exports = (env) => {
  webpack.init(env);

  // Customize webpack config here
  webpack.chainWebpack(config => {
    // Add custom alias or loader
  });

  return webpack.resolveConfig();
};"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Production Builds and Snapshotting"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"For production deployments, you should always build with the '--bundle' and '--env.snapshot' flags (where supported by the platform). Bundling ensures that your code is packed efficiently, while V8 snapshotting drastically reduces Android cold-start times by serializing the initial heap state."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"ns build android --bundle --env.snapshot --env.production"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for NativeScript Bundling"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep dependencies lean and ensure third-party packages are compatible with tree-shaking."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use lazy loading for app modules or navigation routes to decrease initial load time."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Test production builds regularly on physical devices to catch optimization issues early."}]
